STEP 1 - vlan4 of SWITCH_A routed to vlan3 of DUT; directed-broadcast on vlan3;
         verify packets are routed and forwarded from SWITCH_A to DUT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
### control: ip directed-broadcast DISABLED on DUT vlan3 (expect DROP) ###
u4(config-if)# no ip directed-broadcast
u5# ping 10.10.30.31 source 10.10.50.1 repeat 5
PING 10.10.30.31 (10.10.30.31) from 10.10.50.1 : 56(84) bytes of data.
--- 10.10.30.31 ping statistics ---
5 packets transmitted, 0 received, 100% packet loss, time 4157ms
host(vlan3) received (disabled): 0   [expect 0]
  => with directed-broadcast off, the DUT does NOT convert the off-link packet - dropped.

### ip directed-broadcast ENABLED on DUT vlan3 (expect FORWARD) ###
u4(config-if)# ip directed-broadcast
u5# ping 10.10.30.31 source 10.10.50.1 repeat 10
PING 10.10.30.31 (10.10.30.31) from 10.10.50.1 : 56(84) bytes of data.
--- 10.10.30.31 ping statistics ---
10 packets transmitted, 0 received, 100% packet loss, time 9341ms
host(vlan3) received (enabled): 10 of 10   [expect 10]
   IP 10.10.50.1 > 10.10.30.31: ICMP echo request, id 32504, seq 1, length 64
   IP 10.10.50.1 > 10.10.30.31: ICMP echo request, id 32504, seq 2, length 64
   IP 10.10.50.1 > 10.10.30.31: ICMP echo request, id 32504, seq 3, length 64

u4# show running-config interface vlan3
interface vlan3
 ip address 10.10.30.1/27
 ip directed-broadcast

  => The off-link directed broadcast (source 10.10.50.1, a DIFFERENT subnet from the target)
     is routed SWITCH_A -> DUT and delivered onto vlan3: the host sees all 10 as
     10.10.50.1 > 10.10.30.31. This confirms "ip directed-broadcast has nothing to do with
     off-link directed-broadcast packets; these are routed and forwarded to the next
     router/L3 switch," and are then broadcast onto the target subnet only when
     ip directed-broadcast is enabled there. PASS.
